  "query_preview": "..\n\nCloud shield has overspread a good portion of Southeast Michigan \ntoday.  The clouds originated with yet another episode of deep \nconvection across Illinois and Indiana this morning.  The persistent \nribbon of deep moisture and reduced moist stability continues to \nlurk just to the south with peripheral influence in the form of a \ngradual increase in near surface moisture and expanding cloud cover. \nMeanwhile, the warmest conditions are observed across the Thumb and \nSaginaw Valley, where sunny skies remain.\n\nA weak zone of stretching deformation below 700-mb is draped along \nthe southern border counties.  It is currently supported by \nunbalanced flow through increasing system-relative cyclonic flow and \nis maintaining forcing for light shower development, which is \nexpected to continue through the balance of the daylight hours. \nFurthermore, a weak MCV just south of Lake Michigan will move \neastward along this zone - potentially augmenting the ongoing \nactivity into this evening.  The response will be muted in \nintensity, though, due to the lack of instability. \n\nLooking upstream, a shortwave trough over Montana and the southern \nPrairie provinces will slide toward the region tonight. As this wave \napproaches, it will start to interact with low-level frontal zone \nacross the corn belt.  One result will be the southwestward \nextension of the upper-jet and the induction of below entrance \nregion ascent.  Coincidentally, the moist unstable airmass over the \nmid-Mississippi Valley will claw northeast across the Lower \nPeninsula tomorrow morning in a corridor ahead of the wave and the \napproaching cold front.  Some residual upstream shower activity may \nsurvive along the leading instability gradient as it advects into \nSoutheast Michigan during the early to mid morning.  Otherwise, \nscattered showers and thunderstorms will develop across Lower \nMichigan tomorrow afternoon into the evening. The best support for \ncoverage will be closer to the main shortwave across central Lower \nMichigan into the Thumb region. Instability source depth will be \nrestricted to the lowest 1-km, with not much CAPE depth to govern a \nhigher predictability zone for sustained development. Furthermore, \nthe lack of deep layer shear projects the lack of convective \norganization - aside from any activity that is forced along cold \nfrontal zone.\n\nIn the wake of the cold front, Monday and the first half Tuesday \nlook quiet and seasonable.\n\nA broad region of height falls is expected across eastern Canada \ninto the Lakes region and the northeast going into midweek. All the \nglobal ensemble families are trending toward lower with height \nprojections in the context of reduced intra-family spread - garnering \ngreater confidence in a meaningful pattern shift.  The onset of the \ncooler conditions will be prefaced by a cold front with some showers \nand storms possibly as early as Tuesday afternoon.  Chances are \nlingering into early Wednesday, as the timing of the front is still \ngetting sorted out.  An early arrival would offer greater \nthunderstorm chances during peak heating hours on Tuesday, while a \ndelayed arrival would place the region in the nocturnal minima for \nconvection.  The replacement airmass will be cooler and drier with \nhighs in the 70s and dewpoints in the low-mid 50s.",
